@CycleYe Thanks for forwarding the update from MySQL team. Let me first figure out the key point.

I see that you claimed after update statement with "WHERE TRUE" predicate, there should not be any records that are different from the updated fields. You may take a look at this case.

```sql
/* init */ drop table if exists t;
/* init */ create table t(a int, b int);
/* init */ insert into t values (0, 0), (1, 1), (2, 2);

/* s1 */ begin;
/* s1 */ select * from t; -- [(0, 0), (1, 1), (2, 2)]
/* s2 */ begin;
/* s2 */ delete from t where b = 1;
/* s2 */ commit;
/* s1 */ select * from t; -- [(0, 0), (1, 1), (2, 2)]
/* s1 */ update t set a = 10 where true;
/* s1 */ select * from t;  -- [(10, 0), (1, 1), (10, 2)]
/* s1 */ commit;
```

Run this case in MySQL 5.7, MySQL 8.0, and TiDB will get the same result. Because update statement uses current read and is not able to see the already-deleted values, that leads to a reasonable case that after update with "WHERE TRUE" predicate, there may still be some records look weird.

----

So I revised the rule for internal read, the key point may be **"select after update will see the updated value even if update statement did not touch the records and the matched records exist"**, that's a quite complex rule, @CycleYe what do you think?

I do concern about the complexity of this rule. By now, I do not find the weird cases with this rule, but I need further consideration.

